2 “The representation of joy and delight in Literature is often
unconvincing.”
In the light of this view, consider ways in which writers present
intense pleasure. In your answer, compare one drama and one
poetry text from the above lists.

It might seem peculiar to consider Milton’s Christian Epic Paradise Lost
(1667) and John Webster’s Jacobean Tragedy The Duchess of Malfi (1614)
with regard to this question as neither text is one that we would
immediately associate with “intense pleasure” but, on reflection, I think
it can be seen that “joy and delight” feature centrally in both texts.

If “joy and delight” are represented in an “unconvincing” manner in
these texts, it might be a function of the fact that both writers are
somewhat wary of the moral effect of intensely pleasurable experiences.
This is particularly evident in Eve’s peculiar attitude to the forbidden
fruit when she is being seduced by Satan in Book IX:

…Meanwhile the hour of noon drew on, and waked
An eager appetite, raised by the smell
So savoury of that fruit, which with desire,
Inclinable now grown to touch or taste,
Solicited her longing eye…
(Paradise Lost, IX.739-743)

Both of these texts are dominated by strong prohibitions: pleasurable
experiences often seem to be regarded by these early modern writers as
being unethical. This prohibition is strongly visible in Paradise Lost. One
of the only instructions given to Adam and Eve is not to consume the
forbidden fruit. This leads to their ultimate moral downfall with the
introduction of Death and Sin into the world in Book X. These two
personified characters which are introduced by Milton serve as ways to
show the seemingly irreparable damage done by breaking this
prohibition. This rigid attitude towards prohibition correlates with the
strongly deferential society of the early modern period. For readers
contemporary to Milton, the misery that comes with the world-altering
transformation that is the consequence of ignoring prohibition is not
only natural but entirely deserved. The growing religion of Protestantism
was much more focused on a stripped back approach to worship: the
opulence and wealth of the Catholic Church was frequently scorned in
English culture at the time. Satan is described as having ‘Carbuncle[s]’
for eyes and a ‘Neck of verdant Gold’ the wealth and excess of
Catholicism is contrasted with the plainness of honest, straightforward
Protestantism. Satan and his possessions and delights are shown as
distractions from God. The overall message is that prohibitions are
natural and good and should be enforced and those who break them are
strictly undeserving of “joy or delight”. There is a tone of superficiality
to the postlapsarian joy we see. We can see similarly in The Duchess of
Malfi: when the Duchess breaks one of the rules set for her, she swiftly
meets her death. However, this is treated entirely differently. The
Cardinal and Ferdinand are shown to be undoubtedly villains who
engage in sinful behaviour such as adultery and lycanthropic murder.
Rather than arguing for prohibition, The Duchess of Malfi seems to suggest
that some things overrule this including love. Love is undoubtedly a
source of joy and delight for the Duchess and Antonio: rather than it
seeming hollow, or like something that should be prohibited, it is
something that is glorified and compared to divinity. The joy expressed
in The Duchess of Malfi around the area of love is not only convincing it is
frequently shown as right and correct. This attitude towards prohibition
in the texts shows a completely contrasting overall presentation of joy
and delight in these distinct texts.

Significantly, death seems to be conjoined with “intense pleasure” in
these texts.

1 “Literature is often at its most powerful when characters’
motives are obscure and the meaning of the text is, therefore,
ambiguous.”
In the light of this view, consider ways in which writers explore
ambiguity. In your answer, compare one drama and one poetry
text from the above lists.

Some characters in John Webster’s Jacobean Tragedy The Duchess of Malfi
(1614) and John Milton’s Christian Epic Paradise Lost (1667) seem to
intentionally mislead the other characters in the text and, therefore, the
audience and readers of these texts. This renders the works in which
these characters appear as ambiguous.

Bosola as an “intelligencer” is ambiguous to others as a character but, as
the play develops, he is revealed to the audience as that stock character
of the early modern stage, the Malcontent. His whole character depends
on the employment of dramatic irony by Webster – generally, we see
more of Bosola’s true character than those who share the stage with him
(though he is briskly characterized by Antonio in the play’s first scene as
“The only court gall”, I.i.23). However, in the incredibly fast-paced final
scene of the play, we can see that his motivations are more complicated
than his status as a Malcontent would seem to imply - he is, perhaps,
more moral, and more ambiguous, than we originally imagined him to
be. This complicated and often contradictory role is highly interesting to
an audience - Bosola has the most lines throughout the play and often
dominates certain productions (e.g. Arthur Hughes as Bosola in the
2024 Sam Wanamaker Theatre production is profoundly ambiguous and
is a central figure onstage, remaining in the wings throughout the
action).

Satan additionally uses the same kind of trickery on Eve but he seems
less ambiguous initially to a reader than Bosola does. Satan’s character is,
after all, traditionally seen straightforwardly as “the enemy of mankind”,
but any reader of Milton’s poem who knows anything about the text’s
critical hinterland knows that he has traditionally been viewed by critics
in radically different ways. A huge gulf separates Blake’s Milton – a
glorious rebel - from the moral stupidity of Satan as characterized by the
Christian apologist C.S. Lewis.

Some characters in these texts seem completely unambiguous but are, in
fact, more complex than they first appear. Satan, for example, in Paradise
Lost frequently declares how he feels in the poem. Upon arriving in
Eden in Book IX, he tells us that earth is superior to heaven –

O earth, how like to heaven, if not preferred
More justly, seat worthier of gods…
(Paradise Lost, IX.99-100)

However, he frequently seems to be rationalising his own feelings and
this creates an interesting gap between his stated motivations and his
true feelings about any given situation: in the above example, he clearly
claims to prefer earth to heaven (an extremely blasphemous sentiment
because, through his own actions, heaven is lost to him forever).
Similarly, Eve and Adam's motivation for eating the apple is quite clearly
explained by them but this is also buried under post-facto rationalisation
(“…Perhaps thou shalt not die, perhaps the fact/Is not so heinous
now…” Paradise Lost, IX.928-929, says Adam to Eve after she has eaten
the fruit, hoping desperately that God had not really cared that much
about their eating of the forbidden fruit)…

- For Adam his action in eating the fruit is explained, post-
facto, as doing it for love which covers over any more selfish motivation
that he may have

- For Eve, she does it out of a feeling of insecurity but also
undoubtedly does it out of a desire for equality (“…for inferior who is
free...”, Paradise Lost, IX.825). Her true motivation is obscured by the
rush of different reasons and feelings she experiences. She is trying to
convince herself of her righteousness after committing the act.

- Some characters are clear with their motivations, but this does not
always relate to moral righteousness
	- Both Ferdinand and the Cardinal are driven by pretty clear
	instincts that leave little room for ambiguity
		- Power and status for the Cardinal
		- Incestuous sickness and desire for Ferdinand. Jealousy and
		rage.
	- However, the motivations of Antonio and the Duchess are pretty
	clear too…
		- They act on the basis of love and due to the fact they believe
		agency is their right. Despite the treatment of the Duchess by her
		brothers, she maintains this attitude clearly.
		
- Overall, PL seems more concerned with this idea than DOM. The
moral ideas of DOM perhaps benefit from clear bad and clear good
much more than PL. DOM really holds Bosola as its only deeply
unambiguous character whereas PL complicates all of its characters
motives and ideals.
